{"data":{"updated_timestamp":"31/10/2026 14:40:03","profile":{"player_id":"72440","last_updated":"31/10/2026 14:40","fullname":"Liam Alexander Hamilton Scott","short_display_name":"L Scott","name":"Liam Scott","gender":"M","dob":"12/12/2000","date_of_death":null,"place_birth":"Australia","nationality_id":"1","nationality":"Australia","nationality_short_code":"AUS","nick_name":null,"player_skill":{"id":"3","text":"All Rounder","role":"All Rounder"},"batting_style":{"id":"1","text":"Right Handed"},"bowling_style":{"id":"10","text":"Right-arm fast medium"},"writeup":"<p>Liam Scott is a right-arm fast-medium bowling all-rounder who has become a cornerstone of South Australia&rsquo;s domestic revival. After making his Sheffield Shield debut for the Redbacks in November 2019, Scott was selected in Australia&rsquo;s 2020 Under-19 World Cup squad. He broadened his profile over subsequent seasons, making his Big Bash League debut for the Adelaide Strikers in late 2020 and his List A debut for South Australia in early 2022.</p>\r\n<p><br />\r\nScott&rsquo;s major career breakthrough came in 2024&ndash;25 when he was named the One-Day Cup Player of the Tournament, playing a key role as South Australia secured an unprecedented domestic double (Shield and One-Day Cup). He reached even greater heights in 2025&ndash;26, registering a then career-best 5/33 against Queensland alongside his maiden first-class century (147 against Tasmania). His 28 wickets and 547 runs helped South Australia retain the Sheffield Shield and earned Scott the 2025&ndash;26 Sheffield Shield Player of the Year award. Additionally, an elevation up the order in BBL|15 saw him contribute 310 runs for the Strikers, earning a spot in the BBL Team of the Tournament.</p>\r\n<p><br />\r\nHis rapid multi-format ascent earned him an Australia call-up in mid-2026, culminating in his international debut during the ODI tour against Bangladesh in June, where he took 2/57. Following his international debut, Scott joined 